  "study_sample_4": "kache daemon stopped during Nano builds: Nano 118.741 s, kache 115.399 s; harness-ui 29.10 s versus 25.66 s.",
  "open_question": "The study and pairs runs penalize the implementation whose previous build is furthest in the past. Nano is more sensitive to that gap than kache (about +6 s versus about +3 s in the pairs data); the mechanism is not identified. Its effect sits inside rustc execution of harness-ui and the other recompiled crates, not in Nano bookkeeping.",
  "study_sample_5": "Rotation offset 1 (kache builds the shared edit first, Nano third): Nano 118.900 s, kache 117.763 s; harness-ui 27.34 s versus 25.95 s. Nano built the leaf edit first and lost it, 88.401 s versus 81.958 s, with its final link 13 s slower than in every other position. Its revert remained faster (6.258 s versus 13.728 s).",
  "rustc_samples": "Three 3-second macOS samples of a slow harness-ui rustc show ordinary type checking, trait selection and LLVM work; no time blocked in pipe writes, jobserver reads or I/O.",
  "host_contention": "Other-process CPU outside the benchmark tree during shared-edit builds: samples 2-4 Nano 723/736/631%, kache 672/698/644%; sample 5 Nano 271%, kache 103%.",
